<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="three-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="three-title">Three reconciliations</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="deliver-grid">
      <article><h3>POS to tender, daily</h3><p>Match the POS day-end report with cash counted and card and UPI batches. Investigate cancelled and discounted bills, as these are common places for leakage.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Platforms to bank, by settlement</h3><p>Match delivery orders with each payout. Separate commission, ads, penalties and tax so that you can see true income.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Purchases to kitchen, weekly</h3><p>Check vendor bills against goods received and rates agreed. Many restaurants buy daily and record monthly, so errors build up.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="cost-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="cost-title">The one number: food cost</h2>
      <div class="formula">Food cost % = (Opening stock + Purchases − Closing stock) ÷ Food sales × 100</div>
      <p class="para">This needs a real closing stock count. Without one, you are only measuring purchases. Check it monthly, and investigate any jump before blaming suppliers: consider wastage, staff meals, portion size, theft or missing bills.</p>
      <p class="para">Benchmarks vary by concept, so track your own trend first.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>GST points to confirm</h2>
      <p>As reported by secondary sources, and to be checked with your adviser:</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Many restaurants pay 5% without input tax credit</li>
        <li>A restaurant in "specified premises", linked to hotel room tariffs above ₹7,500 a day, is treated differently, at 18% with credit</li>
        <li>On food ordered through a delivery platform, the platform generally pays the GST under section 9(5)</li>
        <li>Those orders therefore need to be reported separately from dine-in and takeaway sales in your GST returns</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="month-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="month-title" class="first-h2">What to hand your accountant each month</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>POS monthly summary, with discounts, cancellations and tender types</li>
        <li>Delivery platform statements and fee invoices</li>
        <li>Vendor bills and payment details, including cash purchases with vouchers</li>
        <li>Closing stock count of main items</li>
        <li>Staff attendance, wages and advances</li>
        <li>Rent, electricity, gas and repairs</li>
        <li>Any licences or renewal fees paid, such as FSSAI</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="problems">Where restaurants' books go wrong</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cash purchases with no bill.</strong> Vegetables and small items are often bought in cash. Use a simple voucher, and pay by traceable means where you can.</li>
        <li><strong>Booking platform payouts as sales.</strong> This understates turnover and hides fees.</li>
        <li><strong>Staff meals and wastage ignored,</strong> so that food cost looks unexplained.</li>
        <li><strong>Tips and service charges unclear.</strong> Decide how they are handled, and record it.</li>
        <li><strong>Payroll in cash, without records.</strong> It is hard to defend later. See {% routeLink "/services/payroll-services/", "payroll" %}.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Have you worked with restaurants on my POS and delivery platforms?</li>
        <li>How do you report dine-in and delivery sales separately for GST?</li>
        <li>Will you give me monthly food cost and labour cost?</li>
        <li>How do you handle my cash purchases?</li>
      </ul>

